(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-acid-base-part-1-vignettes"]={"title":"Acid-Base Part I Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has type 2 diabetes treated with metformin, hypertension treated with a thiazide diuretic, glaucoma treated with an oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, and heartburn for which she takes a calcium carbonate antacid. Her kidney function has worsened, and serum ketones are negative. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.26 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 23 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 10 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 139 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 101 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which of her medicines best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["A carbonic anhydrase inhibitor","Metformin","Her thiazide diuretic","Her calcium carbonate antacid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low pH with a low bicarbonate is metabolic acidosis, and the gap is 139 - (101 + 10) = 28, above 9 to 15: elevated. Metformin is one of the elevated-gap causes (the M of the mnemonic), and negative ketones argue against ketoacidosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with cirrhosis and an albumin of 1.9 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.0 g/dL) has a metabolic acidosis, a chloride of 110 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L) and an anion gap of 11 (normal 9 to 15). Which finding most limits how much the normal gap can reassure you?","choices":["A low unmeasured cation level","Her low serum albumin","Her metabolic acidosis itself","Her high chloride"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Albumin accounts for most of the normal anion gap, so hypoalbuminemia lowers the gap and can hide added unmeasured anions. A normal gap does not exclude a high-gap acidosis and should not override clinical concern. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with a normal-gap metabolic acidosis from a ureteral diversion has a bicarbonate of 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). By the Winter formula, what carbon dioxide range is expected if she is compensating appropriately?","choices":["19 to 23 mm Hg","20 to 24 mm Hg","27 to 31 mm Hg","36 to 44 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 14 + 8 = 21 + 8 = 29, plus or minus 2, so 27 to 31 mm Hg. The formula needs only the bicarbonate, whatever the anion gap is or its cause. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has vomited repeatedly for three days and is dizzy on standing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.50 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 35 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); potassium 3.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L), chloride 91 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.50 is alkalemia, and the bicarbonate of 35 is high and matches it, so it is metabolic. The carbon dioxide of 47 is respiratory compensation (about 0.75 per 1 mEq/L rise). Low potassium and chloride with orthostatic dizziness fit vomiting, which loses hydrochloric acid. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with diabetic ketoacidosis has a bicarbonate of 10 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L) and a carbon dioxide of 22 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). By the Winter formula, how is her respiratory compensation?","choices":["Appropriate","Too little: added respiratory acidosis","Too much: added respiratory alkalosis","It cannot be judged without the gap"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 10 + 8 = 23, plus or minus 2, so 21 to 25. Her measured 22 falls inside that range, so this is a simple, appropriately compensated metabolic acidosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with Crohn disease has had profuse watery diarrhea for five days. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.31 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 31 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 137 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 112 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis","Elevated anion gap metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.31 is acidemia, and the bicarbonate of 15 is low and matches it: metabolic. Gap = 137 - (112 + 15) = 10, within 9 to 15, with a high chloride, so it is a normal-gap (hyperchloremic) acidosis. Bicarbonate loss in diarrhea fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with osteoarthritis has been taking large amounts of aspirin; she also has hypertension treated with a thiazide diuretic and type 2 diabetes treated with metformin. She is breathing fast. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.52 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 28 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 22 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which of her medicines best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["Her thiazide diuretic","Metformin","The combination of diuretic and metformin","Aspirin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. High pH with a low carbon dioxide of 28 is respiratory alkalosis, and her bicarbonate of 22 has barely fallen. Salicylates are a listed toxic cause of hyperventilation, and her fast breathing fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with a severe asthma attack is tiring, and her carbon dioxide has risen over an hour to 60 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). Using an acute bicarbonate rise of about 1 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg rise in carbon dioxide, from midpoints of 40 and 24, what bicarbonate is expected?","choices":["24 mEq/L","26 mEq/L","31 mEq/L","34 mEq/L"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carbon dioxide rose 20 (40 to 60); acutely bicarbonate rises about 1 per 10, so 24 + 2 = 26 mEq/L. Asthma is a listed cause of acute respiratory acidosis, and a near-normal bicarbonate marks an acute process. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with myasthenia gravis is hypoventilating, and her carbon dioxide is rising. Following water + carbon dioxide <-> carbonic acid <-> hydrogen ion + bicarbonate, what happens to her hydrogen ion concentration?","choices":["It rises, lowering her pH","It falls, raising her pH","It is unchanged; bicarbonate absorbs it","It rises once the kidneys respond"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Added carbon dioxide drives the carbonic acid reaction to the right, forming more carbonic acid and then more hydrogen ion and bicarbonate, so pH falls: hypoventilation, raised carbon dioxide, acidemia. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has a metabolic alkalosis with normal blood pressure; she denies vomiting, diuretics and antacids. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 46 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 32 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which test separates the saline-responsive from the saline-unresponsive type?","choices":["Winter formula","Serum albumin","Urine chloride","Anion gap"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Urine chloride differentiates the two types: it is low (below 25 mEq/L) in saline-responsive alkalosis and high (above 40 mEq/L) in saline-unresponsive alkalosis. Her history gives no obvious cause, so the test sorts the list. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man in septic shock has a lactic acidosis. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.27 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 25 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 11 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). How does his acidemia affect oxygen release from hemoglobin to his tissues?","choices":["Release is unchanged by pH","Release increases; the curve shifts right","Release decreases; the curve shifts left","Release decreases; the curve shifts right"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acidosis decreases hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen and shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the right, so hemoglobin releases oxygen more readily and delivery to the tissues increases. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with chronic kidney failure who has missed several dialysis sessions is nauseated and fatigued. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.26 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 29 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 13 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 100 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Elevated anion gap metabolic acidosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.26 is acidemia, and the bicarbonate of 13 is low and matches it: metabolic. Gap = 138 - (100 + 13) = 25, above 9 to 15, so it is elevated; uremia is an elevated-gap cause. The carbon dioxide of 29 is compensation (Winter 1.5 x 13 + 8 = 27.5, plus or minus 2). — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman with myasthenia gravis has worsening weakness and shallow, labored breathing over a day. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.27 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 57 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 26 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Acute respiratory acidosis","Chronic respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.27 is acidemia, and the carbon dioxide of 57 is high and matches it (respiratory). Bicarbonate rose only about 1 per 10 mm Hg (17 above 40 gives about 26), so it is acute. Myasthenia gravis is a listed neuromuscular cause of acute respiratory acidosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with known hypercalcemia has a metabolic alkalosis, a high urine chloride and a normal blood pressure. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.47 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 45 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 32 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). In which group of metabolic alkalosis does she belong?","choices":["Saline responsive","Post-hypercapnic","Saline unresponsive, normotensive","Saline unresponsive, hypertensive"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A high urine chloride (above 40 mEq/L) marks saline-unresponsive alkalosis; with a normal blood pressure it is the normotensive group, which lists hypoparathyroidism and hypercalcemia and severe potassium depletion. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease was intubated with a carbon dioxide of 78 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), and the ventilator brought it to 38 within a few hours. He has had no vomiting, nasogastric suction, diuretics or antacids. The next morning: pH 7.55 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 38 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 32 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What best explains his alkalemia?","choices":["Post-hypercapnic metabolic alkalosis","Acute respiratory alkalosis","Renal compensation that overshot","Worsening chronic respiratory acidosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His kidneys had retained bicarbonate for his chronic hypercapnia; when the carbon dioxide was lowered quickly, that bicarbonate of 32 was left unopposed, a post-hypercapnic metabolic alkalosis. This is why rapid, aggressive carbon dioxide reduction is avoided in chronic respiratory acidosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with heart disease has a severe metabolic alkalosis from vomiting. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.56 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 49 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 43 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). How does her alkalemia affect oxygen delivery to her tissues?","choices":["It shifts the curve to the right","It decreases delivery","It increases delivery","It does not change delivery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alkalosis increases hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen and shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the left, so hemoglobin holds oxygen and delivery to the tissues decreases, a concern with heart disease. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with hypertension treated with a thiazide diuretic is light-headed on standing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.49 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 46 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 34 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); potassium 3.2 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L); urine chloride is low. Along with isotonic saline, which medication change is indicated?","choices":["Add a second diuretic","Start a mineralocorticoid","Start a calcium carbonate antacid","Stop the thiazide diuretic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Low urine chloride and orthostatic symptoms mark a saline-responsive metabolic alkalosis, and diuretic therapy is a listed cause. Treatment is isotonic saline for the volume deficit and discontinuing the diuretic. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man hikes to 4,000 meters and develops headache and breathlessness. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.50 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 29 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 22 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Metabolic alkalosis","Acute respiratory alkalosis","Chronic respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.50 is alkalemia, and the carbon dioxide of 29 is low and matches it (respiratory). Bicarbonate fell about 2 per 10 mm Hg (11 below 40 gives about 22), the acute response. High altitude drives hypoxia-induced hyperventilation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman at 30 weeks of pregnancy has a routine arterial blood gas for mild breathlessness: pH 7.46 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 28 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 19 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Acute respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis","Chronic respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.46 is alkalemia, and the carbon dioxide of 28 is low and matches it: respiratory alkalosis. Bicarbonate fell about 5 per 10 mm Hg (12 below 40 gives about 18 to 19), a chronic renal response. Pregnancy is a listed endocrine cause. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man was ventilated for several days with his carbon dioxide held near 28 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg); the rate was then turned down quickly. The next gas: pH 7.30 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 40 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 19 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What best explains this result?","choices":["Elevated-gap lactic acidosis","Post-hypocapnia metabolic acidosis","Acute respiratory acidosis","Post-hypercapnic metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Days of low carbon dioxide let his kidneys excrete bicarbonate (chronic respiratory alkalosis compensation). Rapid correction left that low bicarbonate of 19 unopposed, a post-hypocapnia metabolic acidosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman began hyperventilating at a high-altitude resort this afternoon. Before her kidneys respond, what is the initial compensation for her respiratory alkalosis?","choices":["The kidneys excrete bicarbonate","She hypoventilates to retain carbon dioxide","The kidneys retain bicarbonate","Cells release hydrogen ions for potassium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The initial response to respiratory alkalosis is cellular secretion of hydrogen ions in exchange for potassium. Later the kidneys excrete bicarbonate and retain chloride, lowering bicarbonate, potassium and raising chloride. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with severe obesity and a high fever from pneumonia has a carbon dioxide of 62 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). What usually causes a raised carbon dioxide like his?","choices":["Excess production from the fever","Excess bicarbonate retained by the kidneys","Hyperventilation","Under-excretion from hypoventilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hypercapnia is usually the result of under-excretion (hypoventilation), not excess production. At steady state carbon dioxide production matches excretion, so a high level means ventilation is not keeping up. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman with a normal-gap metabolic acidosis has a bicarbonate of 18 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). By the Winter formula, what carbon dioxide range is expected with appropriate compensation?","choices":["24 to 28 mm Hg","25 to 29 mm Hg","33 to 37 mm Hg","36 to 44 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 18 + 8 = 27 + 8 = 35, plus or minus 2, so 33 to 37 mm Hg. A value above 37 would add a respiratory acidosis; below 33, a respiratory alkalosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with septic shock and lactic acidosis is tiring. His bicarbonate is 12 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L) and his carbon dioxide is 38 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). By the Winter formula, what does his carbon dioxide show?","choices":["An added respiratory alkalosis","Normal, since 38 is in range","An added respiratory acidosis","Appropriate compensation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: 1.5 x 12 + 8 = 26, plus or minus 2, so 24 to 28. His carbon dioxide of 38 is well above it: metabolic acidosis together with respiratory acidosis, which can signal impending respiratory failure in a tiring patient. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic lung disease has been vomiting for days. His bicarbonate is 40 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L), his pH 7.46 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), and his carbon dioxide 58 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). What does the carbon dioxide level indicate?","choices":["Expected compensation alone","A concurrent respiratory alkalosis","A primary metabolic acidosis","A concurrent primary respiratory acidosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In metabolic alkalosis, respiratory compensation rarely raises carbon dioxide above 55 mm Hg. His 58 is above that (and above 52, the 16 x 0.75 rise from 40), so a concurrent primary respiratory acidosis fits, along with his chronic lung disease. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with long-standing chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a stable chronic respiratory acidosis. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.34 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 60 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 33 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which serum electrolyte finding is expected?","choices":["High chloride","Low bicarbonate","An elevated anion gap","Low chloride"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In chronic respiratory acidosis the kidneys excrete acid as ammonium chloride and retain bicarbonate, so hypochloremia accompanies the raised bicarbonate of 33. This is the renal compensation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man is confused after drinking antifreeze (ethylene glycol). Sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), potassium 4.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L), chloride 102 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 8 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What is his anion gap?","choices":["30","34","38","46"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anion gap = sodium - (chloride + bicarbonate) = 140 - (102 + 8) = 140 - 110 = 30, above the normal 9 to 15: an elevated-gap metabolic acidosis, fitting ethylene glycol, the E of the mnemonic. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman in diabetic ketoacidosis has Kussmaul respirations (deep, rapid breathing). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.22 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 20 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 8 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What does her breathing pattern accomplish?","choices":["It signals impending respiratory failure","It lowers carbon dioxide to offset the acidosis","It retains carbon dioxide to raise her pH","It is a primary respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Metabolic acidosis is compensated by increased ventilation to decrease carbon dioxide; in severe cases hyperventilation is profound, as Kussmaul respirations (increased tidal volume and tachypnea) in diabetic ketoacidosis. Her carbon dioxide of 20 shows it. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes has diabetic ketoacidosis with Kussmaul respirations (deep, rapid breathing). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.19 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 21 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 8 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which approach corrects her acid-base disorder?","choices":["Treat the underlying disorder","Slow her breathing to retain carbon dioxide","Paper bag rebreathing","Bicarbonate until her pH is normal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Elevated-gap metabolic acidosis is treated by treating the underlying disorder; her pH of 7.19 is acidemic but not in the unsustainable range below 6.7. Her deep breathing is compensation and is left alone. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man with type 1 diabetes ran out of insulin. He is vomiting, with Kussmaul respirations (deep, rapid breathing) and a fruity breath odor. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.17 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 20 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 7 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 135 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 99 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Respiratory alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Elevated anion gap metabolic acidosis","Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.17 is acidemia, and the bicarbonate of 7 is low and matches it, so it is metabolic. Anion gap = 135 - (99 + 7) = 29, above 9 to 15, so it is elevated. Kussmaul breathing is the compensation, and diabetic ketoacidosis is an elevated-gap cause. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 55"}]};
